2,"Amerindian Music of Chile: Aymara, Qawashqar, Mapuche",https://folkways.si.edu/ameridian-music-of-chile-aymara-qaqashqar-mapuche/american-indian-world/album/smithsonian,"Aymara musicians traditionally incorporate wind instruments, reed whistles, stringed instruments, and pipes for songs dedicated to animals or saints, or freeform chants. As farmers, the Aymara people’s floreo festival celebrates their herds. Qawashqar tracks presented here are in the form of chants.","Aymara, Southern",Aymaran
3,Aymara Instruments: Exploring the Unique Sounds of Andean Music,https://completeera.com/aymara-instruments-exploring-the-unique-sounds-of-andean-music/,"Aymara instruments are the heart of Andean music, blending raw energy with deep cultural roots. From the haunting zampoña (panpipes) to the rhythmic charango, each instrument tells a story of tradition, spirituality, and resistance.","Aymara, Southern",Aymaran
4,AYMARA MUSIC,https://www.aymara.org/webarchives/www2002/english/waruru.php,"We have made an effort so that the pieces in this list are those composed, arranged and performed by native Aymara bands from a diversity of farming communities in Peru and Bolivia.","Aymara, Southern",Aymaran
5,"EXPERIENCE ETHEREAL AYMARA | ANDES AMBIENT SoundscapesAymara Music - YouTubeAymara – Music and Dance - Chile PrecolombinoAmerindian Music of Chile: Aymara, Qawashqar, MapucheAymara people - Wikipedia",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Quwv8tHeqto,"Jan 19, 2026 · Immerse yourself in the serene yet profound world of traditional Aymara music from the Andes of Peru and Bolivia, where the haunting tones of sikuris blend with the rhythmic essence of... The Aymara ethnic group is quite ancient. They live today in Peru, Bolivia and Chile. They have a different language than the Quechua. In addition to coplas (rhymed verse), Aymara music includes many songs dedicated to llamas and alpacas—animals that are essential to the subsistence of the Aymara people living on the Altiplano. Aymara musicians traditionally incorporate wind instruments, reed whistles, stringed instruments, and pipes for songs dedicated to animals or saints, or freeform chants. As farmers, the Aymara people’s floreo festival celebrates their herds. Qawashqar tracks presented here are in the form of chants. The Aymara or Aimara (Aymara: aymara, listen ⓘ) people are an Indigenous people in the Andes and Altiplano regions of South America. Approximately 2.3 million Aymara live in northwest Argentina, Bolivia, Chile, and Peru. The Aymara have lived in the region for over 7,250 years. Where do Aymaras live? Approximately 2.3 million Aymara live in northwest Argentina, Bolivia, Chile, and Peru. The Aymara have lived in the region for over 7,250 years. In the late 15th century or early 16th century, Aymaras came under the rule of the Inca Empire and later of the Spanish Empire in the 16th century. What is Aymara indigenous spirituality? Aymara Indigenous spirituality (pachakuti) is based on the seasons and key agricultural events. It gives the foundation of the existence of the Aymara and their responsibility to the natural environment. Where did the name Aymara come from? The name of the Aymara people stems from the word Ayma-ra-mi meaning ""a place with many communally owned farms"". The word ""Aymara"" also refers to a group of language dialects of which the origin, spread and time-frame are debated. Who were the Aymara tribes? Rather than one ethnically homogenous group, the Aymara consisted of sometimes warring clans of differing dialects, traditions and geographic distribution. Among them were the Huamallas, Hatuncollas, Chuquitos and Azángaros, and, the Lupaqa and Colla.","Aymara, Southern",Aymaran
